              "expected": 65.28768,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 93
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1e-06
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTankTemperature",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 64.3096,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 96
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1e-06
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_carried_value_itself_is_checked_and_not_only_what_is_published",
          "description": "`sumTempDelta` is the state of the recurrence, and the two equations that read it read it one cell BEHIND the cell they write. So it gets its own assertions rather than being trusted through `tt_qhTankTemperature`. Cell 2's value is -0.22499942780000026 -- this document's own binary64 answer, which is `quarterHourTemperature[2] - quarterHourTankTemperature[2]` and is therefore already pinned by the two assertions above. Cell 95's is the reference's own last tank temperature INVERTED through the smoothing: (64.3096 - 63.7999992371) / 1.4 = 0.364000545, an independent route to the carried value that never reads this file's arithmetic. It gets rel 1e-4 rather than 1e-6 because the inversion divides a six-significant-figure storage error by 1.4 and then relates it to a number three hundred times smaller, which turns 8e-07 into 1e-04 -- that is amplification of the reference's own storage, not disagreement.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhDeltaSum",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.22499942780000026,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 2
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 1e-12
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhDeltaSum",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": 0.36400054492857137,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 95
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "rel": 0.0001
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "the_delta_column_agrees_absolutely_because_it_is_a_cancellation",
          "description": "The reference stores `tempDelta` alongside the tank temperature, and it is the one column here that does NOT agree relatively. It is the difference of two numbers near 63.8 that MOVES holds in a Java 32-bit float, so a 4e-06 absolute rounding on each operand survives into a result of magnitude 0.2 as a 2e-05 relative one. Cell 2 is -0.224998470000 stored against -0.2249994278 computed, 9.6e-07 apart absolutely and 4.3e-06 apart relatively; cell 96 is -0.209602360000 against -0.2095889194, 1.3e-05 apart. Asserting them at `abs 2e-05` is not a widened gate -- it is the correct gate for a cancellation, and the relative gate is the wrong one to apply. docs/esm-conventions.md §10 is the rule; this is its clearest instance in the repository, because the two operands are checked at rel 1e-12 and rel 1e-6 in the tests above and only their difference needs an absolute bound.",
          "time_span": {
            "start": 0.0,
            "end": 0.0
          },
          "assertions": [
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTempDelta",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.22499847,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 2
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 2e-05
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTempDelta",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.13499832,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 3
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 2e-05
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTempDelta",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.17100525,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 4
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 2e-05
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTempDelta",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.28768158,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 93
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 2e-05
              }
            },
            {
              "variable": "tt_qhTempDelta",
              "time": 0.0,
              "expected": -0.20960236,
              "coords": {
                "tt_quarter_hour_rows": 96
              },
              "tolerance": {
                "abs": 2e-05
              }
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "id": "TTG_1c_reproduces_all_24_reference_cold_soak_tank_temperatures",
          "description": "The generator's actual output, every row of it, against the reference's own `ColdSoakTankTemperature` table for zone 261610 month 8. Twenty-four assertions and not a sample, because this is the table both remaining evaporative slices read and because the recurrence's error grows along the axis: hour 1 is the anchor and cannot be wrong, hour 24 is 93 cells downstream. The worst of the 24 is 7.550e-07 relative, which is the column's six-significant-figure storage floor (63.0566 stands for 63.056601127648804) and not accumulated error -- the recurrence's feedback ratio is -0.4, so it CONTRACTS error rather than amplifying it, which is why 93 cells cost nothing. The tank lags the air: the maximum, 81.8977 at hour 17, is 3.6e-03 below the 81.9000015259 ambient maximum which occurs at hours 16 and 17, and the minimum, 59.5723 at hour 7, is 0.072 ABOVE the 59.5 ambient minimum at the same hour. That lag is the whole physical content of TTG-1 and it is what a dropped recurrence would destroy while still returning 24 plausible temperatures.",
